    excerpt: "A single success with your puppy feels like a breakthrough, but one good day does not equal a reliable habit. Learn how to build genuine consistency in puppy behaviour routines.",
    content: "\"One good day is not reliability.\" This quote from the dog training philosophy at the heart of these books is one of the most important concepts for any new puppy owner to internalise. It is also one of the most commonly violated. When your puppy finally makes it through an entire night without an accident, or walks calmly on the lead for the first time, or greets a visitor without jumping, the natural human response is relief and celebration. You feel like the training has worked. You feel like you have turned a corner. And in that moment of triumph, you relax. You loosen the structure. You give the puppy a little more freedom because, after all, he has earned it. This is a mistake, and it is one that countless owners make.\n\nThe problem is that reliability is not a single event. It is a pattern. A puppy who holds his bladder one night has demonstrated capacity, not consistency. A puppy who walks nicely on one walk has shown that he can do it under specific conditions, not that he will do it every time. The difference between capacity and consistency is the difference between a fluke and a habit. And habits, whether good or bad, are built through repetition over time. One repetition is not enough. Ten repetitions might not be enough. The book's philosophy is clear: you need to see the behaviour repeated reliably across different days, different times, different environments, and different levels of distraction before you can consider it established.\n\nThis principle has practical implications for how you manage your puppy's daily routine. If your puppy has one accident-free day, do not expand his access to the house. If your puppy has one good walk, do not lengthen the next walk beyond what the routine prescribes. If your puppy settles calmly one evening, do not assume he will do it again without the same management that produced the calm the first time. The management stays in place until the behaviour is reliable, and reliability is measured in weeks, not days.\n\nConsider potty training as an example. A puppy who goes outside successfully for three consecutive days has had a good streak. That is encouraging. But it is not reliability. The puppy might still have an accident on day four because he was distracted, or on day five because he drank more water than usual, or on day six because his schedule was disrupted by a family event. If you have already expanded his house access based on three good days, that accident happens on a carpet that was supposed to be safe. If you had maintained the original management plan, the accident would have happened on a hard floor that is easy to clean, or it would have been prevented entirely because the puppy was in a managed space.\n\nThe same principle applies to behaviour around other dogs, around food, around visitors, and around children. A puppy who ignores a dropped piece of food once has shown impulse control in that moment. He has not demonstrated that he will ignore food every time. Until you have tested the behaviour repeatedly and seen it hold, you manage the environment. You do not leave food on low surfaces. You do not trust the puppy alone in the kitchen. You maintain the structure.\n\nThe book's advice here is not pessimistic. It is protective. By insisting that one good day is not reliability, it is protecting you from the disappointment of premature celebration followed by a setback. Setbacks are discouraging. They make you feel like you are going backwards when you thought you were going forwards. And that discouragement can erode your consistency, which is the very thing that builds reliability. The cycle looks like this: premature celebration leads to relaxed management, which leads to a setback, which leads to discouragement, which leads to inconsistent training, which delays actual reliability. The way to break the cycle is to maintain your management structure until the behaviour is genuinely reliable, as measured by repeated performance over time.\n\nSo what does reliability actually look like? For a puppy, it means the behaviour happens correctly in the vast majority of repetitions, across different conditions, for a sustained period. For potty training, it might mean no accidents for two weeks straight. For lead walking, it might mean calm walking on ten consecutive walks in different locations. For settling, it might mean the puppy calm on his bed for five consecutive evenings without intervention. These benchmarks are not arbitrary. They represent the number of repetitions required for a behaviour to shift from conscious effort to habitual response. Once the behaviour is habitual, you can begin to relax the management, and the behaviour will hold because it has been deeply reinforced.\n\nThe takeaway for puppy behaviour routines is this: celebrate the good days, but do not change your management based on them. Keep the structure consistent, keep the expectations clear, and wait for the pattern to emerge. When reliability arrives, you will know it not because of one impressive moment, but because the good days stop feeling like surprises and start feeling like the norm. That is the moment to adjust. Not before.",
