    excerpt: "Large breed puppies reach adult size long before they reach adult maturity. Learn how to manage the critical gap between physical size and behavioural readiness in large breed puppy care.",
    content: "\"A bigger dog is not a mature dog.\" This is the companion principle to the idea that growth is not maturity, and it deserves its own focused discussion because the consequences of misunderstanding it are so significant. When you are raising a large breed puppy, the speed at which he grows creates a persistent problem: people respond to his size, not his actual developmental stage. This includes family members, visitors, strangers on walks, and even other dog owners. Everyone sees a big dog and treats him accordingly, and that treatment can be inappropriate, overwhelming, and counterproductive for a puppy who is not yet mature.\n\nThe most common manifestation of this problem is during greetings. A six-month-old German Shepherd puppy approaches someone at the park. He is already large, perhaps sixty pounds, and the person reacts as they would to an adult dog. They reach out confidently, they make direct eye contact, they speak loudly, they may even pat the puppy roughly on the head or grab his collar. For an adult, well-socialised German Shepherd, this interaction might be manageable. For a six-month-old puppy whose social skills are still developing, this interaction can be frightening or overstimulating. The puppy may respond by jumping up, mouthing, barking, or shrinking away. The person then labels the behaviour as a problem — \"that dog is aggressive\" or \"that dog is out of control\" — when in reality the problem is that a puppy was treated as an adult.\n\nThe same dynamic plays out inside the home. A large breed puppy who jumps on the sofa is not doing the same thing as a small breed puppy who jumps on the sofa. The large puppy takes up the entire sofa. He knocks over the coffee table. He intimidates visitors. The family's reaction is often disproportionate because the impact is disproportionate. But the puppy's motivation is the same: he wants to be close to his people, and he has not yet learned that the sofa is off-limits. The solution is not harsher punishment because of his size. The solution is the same management and training that would work for any puppy: prevent access to the sofa until the rule is learned, reward alternative behaviours, and maintain consistency. The size of the dog does not change the training principle. It only changes the urgency.\n\nExercise is another area where the size-maturity gap causes problems. People see a big, energetic puppy and assume he needs to run. They take him jogging, they throw the ball for an hour, they let him sprint around the garden. But the puppy's joints, tendons, and growth plates are not designed for that level of impact. The damage from over-exercising a large breed puppy is cumulative and often invisible until it manifests as joint disease, arthritis, or orthopaedic problems later in life. The book's guidance is clear: exercise the puppy according to his age, not his size. Use the five-minutes-per-month-of-age guideline. Prioritise structured, low-impact activities like lead walks, training sessions, and gentle play. Save the long runs and high-impact activities for after the growth plates have closed.\n\nThe gap between size and maturity also affects how families perceive the puppy's progress. A small breed puppy who is not yet house-trained at five months is seen as normal. A large breed puppy who is not yet house-trained at five months is often seen as a problem, because he produces large accidents and his size makes the mess feel more significant. But the house-training timeline is determined by developmental factors, not breed size. Large breed puppies are not faster or slower to house-train than small breeds. They are puppies, and they learn on a puppy timeline.\n\nThe practical takeaway for large breed puppy care is this: at every stage, ask yourself whether you are responding to the puppy's size or to his actual maturity. If your six-month-old German Shepherd is doing something that would be normal for a six-month-old puppy of any breed, manage it as a puppy behaviour, not as an adult dog problem. Keep the training appropriate for his developmental stage. Keep the exercise appropriate for his growing body. Keep the social interactions appropriate for his social skills. And remember that the gap between his physical size and his behavioural maturity will narrow over time, but it will not close until he is genuinely mature, which for large breeds can be two to three years. Patience during that gap is not optional. It is essential.\n\nGerman Shepherd puppy care requires specific attention to the breed's predisposition to certain health conditions. Hip and elbow dysplasia are common in the breed, and while genetics play a significant role, nutrition and exercise during the growth period can influence the severity and onset of these conditions. Feeding a large breed puppy food formulated to support controlled growth, avoiding excessive calcium supplementation, and following the exercise guidelines for large breed puppies all contribute to healthier joint development. The book recommends discussing breed-specific health risks with your veterinarian early and establishing a monitoring plan that tracks the puppy's growth and development over time.\n\nThe German Shepherd's intelligence is one of the breed's most celebrated traits, but it is also a source of potential problems if not properly channelled. A German Shepherd puppy who is not given adequate mental stimulation will find his own stimulation, and his choices are unlikely to align with your preferences. Destructive chewing, excessive barking, escape attempts, and reactivity are all common outcomes of under-stimulation in this breed. The book's recommendation is to provide at least two structured brain-work sessions per day in addition to physical exercise. Training sessions, puzzle toys, sniff games, and learning new skills in different environments all contribute to a mentally satisfied German Shepherd who is a pleasure to live with.",
