    content: "\"If the honest answer was 'no' or 'not now,' the Reset will tell you that too.\" This is one of the most important caveats in the book's discussion of the puppy blues, and it demonstrates the book's commitment to honesty over reassurance. The system reset is designed to give families a genuine opportunity to succeed with their puppy. But it is also designed to reveal the truth, and the truth is not always that the family should keep the dog. Sometimes the honest answer, even after a well-executed reset, is that the family is not in a position to provide what the puppy needs, now or in the foreseeable future.\n\nThe book describes this possibility without judgment. The reset is a test of the family's system, and like any good test, it produces a genuine result. If the family implements the reset faithfully for two to four weeks and the situation improves significantly, the answer is clear: the family can do this with the right system in place. If the family implements the reset faithfully and the situation does not improve — or improves but remains unmanageable — the answer is also clear: the family's capacity, even with the best possible system, does not match the puppy's needs. This is not a comfortable conclusion, but it is an honest one, and the book argues that honest conclusions are more valuable than false reassurances.\n\nThe scenarios where the reset reveals a \"no\" answer are varied. The puppy may have a behavioural or medical issue that requires expertise and time beyond what the family can provide. The family's circumstances may have changed since the puppy arrived — a new job, a new baby, a health issue, a financial crisis — in ways that fundamentally alter their capacity. The family system may be so fractured that even a structured reset cannot hold, because the underlying interpersonal issues are deeper than the reset can address. The least-prepared person in the family may be suffering in ways that the system cannot alleviate, and their suffering may be the factor that makes continued ownership untenable.\n\nThe book's position is that discovering this through the reset is a good outcome, not a bad one. It is better to discover through a structured, honest process that the situation is not workable than to continue indefinitely in a state of chaos, resentment, and declining welfare for both the family and the dog. The reset gives the family the information they need to make an informed decision, and that information is valuable regardless of what the decision turns out to be.\n\nThe book also addresses the fear that the reset will fail. Many families who are considering the reset are afraid that they will put in the effort and discover that they cannot make it work. This fear can prevent them from trying the reset at all, which means they remain in the current broken state indefinitely. The book's response to this fear is pragmatic: the current state is already a failure state. If the reset reveals that the family cannot succeed, the family has not lost anything that was not already lost. They have gained clarity, which is the prerequisite for a good decision, whether that decision is to continue with the puppy under the new system or to rehome him.\n\nFor families struggling with puppy blues, the message is this: the reset is not a guarantee of success. It is a process that produces honest results. If the result is that you can succeed with the right system, that is wonderful. If the result is that you cannot, that is also valuable information. Either way, you are in a better position to make a good decision than you were before the reset. The reset does not sugar-coat the reality. It reveals it. And that is its greatest strength, because the only thing worse than a difficult truth is a comforting lie that prevents you from acting on the truth until the situation has deteriorated beyond recovery.\n\nThe financial dimension of dog ownership is one that many families underestimate. The initial purchase price or adoption fee is only the beginning. A puppy needs food, veterinary care including vaccinations, flea treatments, worming, and potential emergency visits, grooming supplies, toys that will be destroyed and replaced regularly, a bed, a crate, bowls, leads, collars, harnesses, and training resources. Over the lifetime of a dog, these costs add up to thousands of pounds, sometimes tens of thousands. If the family has not budgeted for these ongoing expenses, the financial pressure becomes another source of stress that undermines the system. Planning for costs is not unromantic — it is responsible. A family that has budgeted for the dog's needs is a family that can provide for them without anxiety.\n\nThe emotional education that children receive from living with a dog is profound and long-lasting. When children participate in the daily care of a family dog, they learn responsibility in a way that no lecture or chore chart can replicate. They learn that another living being depends on them for basic needs like food, water, exercise, and companionship. They learn empathy by observing the dog's emotions and responding to them appropriately. They learn patience through training, which requires consistent repetition and calm persistence. They learn about loss and grief when the dog eventually reaches the end of his life, a difficult but essential human experience. These lessons are not taught explicitly — they are absorbed through daily interaction with a creature who is completely dependent on the family's care.",
