    slug: "family-dog-readiness-real-bottleneck-family-system",
    title: "Family Dog Readiness: The Real Bottleneck Is Often the Family System, Not the Dog",
    excerpt: "When dog ownership goes wrong, the problem is usually the household system, not the dog. Learn why family dog readiness depends on systemic strength, not the puppy's behaviour.",
    content: "\"The real bottleneck is often the family system, not the dog.\" This is one of the most important diagnostic insights in the book, because it reframes how families should think about problems that arise during puppy ownership. When things are going wrong — the puppy is not house-trained, the puppy is destructive, the puppy is pulling on the lead, the household is in chaos — the natural instinct is to look at the puppy as the source of the problem. The puppy is difficult. The puppy is stubborn. The puppy is not trainable. The book argues that in the vast majority of cases, the problem is not the dog. The problem is the system.\n\nThe diagnostic process the book recommends starts with a question: is the system functional? Before attributing any problem to the puppy's inherent traits, examine whether the management system is in place and being followed consistently. Is there a clear daily schedule? Is it being followed? Are responsibilities assigned and fulfilled? Are rules clear and consistently enforced? Is the puppy's environment managed — gates up, doors closed, hazards removed? Is the puppy getting appropriate exercise, mental stimulation, and rest? If the answer to any of these questions is no, the system is broken, and the puppy's behaviour is a symptom of that breakdown, not an independent problem.\n\nThe book provides a framework for diagnosing system failures. If the puppy is having frequent accidents, check: is the potty schedule appropriate and being followed? Is the puppy's house access restricted to manageable areas? Is the family using enzymatic cleaner to remove scent markers? Is someone consistently taking the puppy out at the right times? If any of these elements is missing, the house-training failure is a system failure, not a puppy failure. If the puppy is destructive, check: is the puppy getting enough appropriate chew items? Is the puppy's access to inappropriate items blocked? Is the puppy getting enough exercise and mental stimulation? Is the puppy being supervised when loose in the house? Again, if these elements are missing, the destruction is a system failure.\n\nThe book also addresses the common dynamic where the system was initially functional but has degraded over time. This often happens in the second or third month of ownership, when the initial enthusiasm has faded and the daily grind of puppy management has set in. The schedule slips. The gates get left open. The responsibilities revert to the primary carer. The rules become inconsistent. The puppy, who was doing well under the original system, starts to regress because the system is no longer providing the structure he needs. The family interprets the regression as a problem with the puppy, when in fact it is a problem with the system.\n\nThe book's message for family dog readiness is that the strength of the family system is the single most important predictor of success. A strong system can manage a range of puppy temperaments, including those that are more challenging. A weak system will struggle even with an easy puppy, because the system cannot provide the consistency and structure that all puppies need. The family should invest their preparation time in building the system, not in trying to predict or select the perfect puppy. The system is the variable that is within the family's control, and it is the variable that has the greatest impact on outcomes.\n\nThe practical advice is to audit your system regularly. Every few weeks, review the schedule, the responsibilities, the rules, and the management infrastructure. Ask: is this system still functional? Are all elements being followed consistently? Has the family's capacity changed? Has the puppy's needs changed? Adjust the system as needed. This ongoing maintenance is what keeps the system functional over time, and a functional system is what keeps the puppy well-behaved, the household manageable, and the family's relationship with the dog positive and rewarding. The bottleneck is not the dog. It is the system. Fix the system, and the dog will follow.\n\nThe role of extended family and regular visitors in the family dog system is often overlooked but critically important. Grandparents, aunts, uncles, family friends, and neighbours who visit regularly all interact with the dog, and their behaviour affects the system. A grandparent who slips the dog treats under the table undermines the family's feeding rules. A friend who encourages the dog to jump up during greetings reinforces a behaviour the family is trying to eliminate. A neighbour who lets the dog out without telling anyone disrupts the potty schedule. The system owner needs to brief regular visitors on the house rules and explain why they matter. Most people will respect the rules once they understand that they are part of a structured approach to raising a well-behaved dog.\n\nFamily meeting protocols are an essential component of the family dog system that many families skip. A regular check-in — weekly or biweekly — where every family member discusses the puppy's progress, raises concerns, and suggests adjustments to the system is one of the most effective tools for preventing resentment and maintaining consistency. These meetings do not need to be long. Ten or fifteen minutes is sufficient. What matters is that every voice is heard, that problems are addressed promptly, and that the system evolves as the puppy grows and the family's circumstances change. Families that meet regularly are far more likely to maintain a functional system and far less likely to experience the kind of accumulated frustration that leads to crisis.",
