    title: "Puppy Behaviour Routines: Calm Is More Important Than Excitement — Why Tranquillity Wins",
    excerpt: "Most owners celebrate an excited, bouncy puppy. But calm behaviour is the foundation of a well-adjusted dog. Learn why calm trumps excitement in puppy behaviour routines.",
    content: "\"Calm is more important than excitement.\" This statement challenges the dominant cultural narrative about what a \"good dog\" looks like. In the popular imagination, a good dog is an excited dog: tail wagging, tongue out, bouncing with enthusiasm, ready for action. The book argues that while excitement has its place, calm is the more important quality, because calm is the foundation upon which all other desirable behaviours are built. A calm dog can learn, can focus, can settle, can be trusted in a wide range of environments, and can live harmoniously within a family. An excited dog who cannot calm down is a dog who is difficult to train, difficult to manage, and difficult to live with, regardless of how much enthusiasm he displays.\n\nThe book explains this principle in terms of the puppy's nervous system. Excitement and calm are two ends of a spectrum of arousal. A puppy at the high-arousal end is in a state of activation: his heart rate is elevated, his muscles are tense, his attention is scattered, and his ability to process information and make good decisions is impaired. A puppy at the low-arousal end is in a state of relaxation: his heart rate is normal, his muscles are soft, his attention is focused, and his ability to learn and respond to cues is optimal. Training happens most effectively at the calm end of the spectrum. Behaviour management happens most effectively at the calm end of the spectrum. Social interactions happen most appropriately at the calm end of the spectrum.\n\nThe book's daily routine is designed to keep the puppy's arousal level within an optimal range. High-arousal activities like vigorous play are included, but they are bounded in duration and always followed by calm-down periods. The transitions between activities are structured to bring the puppy's arousal level down before moving to the next phase of the day. The evening routine is specifically designed to trend toward calm, ending with a settled, quiet period before bedtime.\n\nThe book also addresses the owner's role in the puppy's arousal level. Puppies are mirrors of their owners' emotional state. If the owner is excited — greeting the puppy with high energy, speaking in a high-pitched voice, moving quickly — the puppy matches that energy. If the owner is calm — greeting the puppy quietly, speaking in a normal tone, moving steadily — the puppy receives the signal that the situation is normal and manageable. The book recommends that owners practice calm, low-energy interactions as the default, reserving high-energy interactions for specific play sessions. This approach teaches the puppy that calm is the baseline state and excitement is a temporary deviation, not the other way around.\n\nThe practical implications for the family are significant. A puppy who defaults to calm is easier to manage in the house. He does not destroy things. He does not bark excessively. He does not overwhelm visitors. He can be left alone for short periods without distress. He is pleasant to be around, and his presence enhances the family's quality of life rather than disrupting it. These outcomes are not achieved by suppressing the puppy's personality or energy. They are achieved by structuring the puppy's environment and routine to build calm as the dominant state, with excitement as an appropriate and enjoyable part of the day that is bounded and followed by recovery.\n\nThe book's message is a reorientation of what families should value in their puppy. Do not value excitement as the primary indicator of a happy, well-adjusted dog. Value calm. A calm puppy is a puppy whose nervous system is regulated, whose behaviour is appropriate, and whose future as a well-behaved adult dog is secure. Build calm through structure, consistency, and low-arousal interactions, and the excitement will come naturally during play and activity. Calm is more important than excitement, and the families that internalise this principle are the families that raise the most pleasant, well-behaved dogs.\n\nUnderstanding canine body language is one of the most valuable skills a dog owner can develop. Dogs communicate primarily through their bodies, not through vocalisation. A wagging tail does not always mean a happy dog. The position of the tail, the tension in the body, the position of the ears, the shape of the eyes, and the overall posture all convey information about the dog's emotional state and his likely next behaviour. A dog who is stiff, still, and staring is not relaxed — he is likely in a state of high arousal that could lead to a reactive response. A dog who turns his head away, licks his lips, or yawns in a non-sleepy context is signalling discomfort or anxiety. Learning to read these signals allows the owner to intervene before a behaviour escalates, which is far more effective than reacting after the behaviour has already occurred.\n\nThe role of nutrition in behaviour is often underestimated by new puppy owners. A puppy who is fed an inappropriate diet, or who is fed at irregular intervals, may exhibit behavioural problems that are directly related to nutritional factors rather than training issues. Blood sugar fluctuations caused by irregular feeding can lead to irritability, difficulty concentrating during training, and increased scavenging behaviour. Overfeeding can cause lethargy and digestive discomfort. Underfeeding can cause hunger-driven behaviours like chewing, scavenging, and excessive barking. Establishing a consistent feeding schedule with the right food in the right amounts is one of the simplest and most effective ways to support good behaviour in your puppy.",
    topic: "Dog Behaviour",
